{"actor":{"avatar_url":"https:\\/\\/avatars.githubusercontent.com\\/u\\/45853521?","display_login":"pfeatherstone","gravatar_id":"","id":"45853521","login":"pfeatherstone","url":"https:\\/\\/api.github.com\\/users\\/pfeatherstone"},"created_at":"2021-01-02T16:37:27Z","id":"14690746728","payload":{"action":"closed","issue":{"assignees":[],"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","body":"This would be similar to ```torch.cat``` or ```numpy.concatenate```.\\r\\n\\r\\nHere is the code. Would anyone find this useful as a PR? I would.\\r\\n\\r\\n```\\r\\ntemplate <int axis, typename M1, typename M2>\\r\\nstruct cat_op \\r\\n{    \\r\\n    const M1& m1;\\r\\n    const M2& m2;\\r\\n    \\r\\n    static_assert(axis == 0 or axis == 1, \\"bad axis value\\");\\r\\n    static_assert(axis == 0 ? M1::NC == M2::NC or M1::NC == 0 or M2::NC == 0 : M1::NR == M2::NR or M1::NR == 0 or M2::NR == 0, \\"bad dims\\");\\r\\n    static const long cost = M1::cost + M2::cost;\\r\\n    static const long NR   = M1::NR == 0 or M2::NR == 0 ? 0 : (axis == 0 ? M1::NR + M2::NR : M1::NR);\\r\\n    static const long NC   = M1::NC == 0 or M2::NC == 0 ? 0 : (axis == 0 ? M1::NC : M1::NC + M2::NC);\\r\\n    typedef typename M1::type             type;\\r\\n    typedef typename M1::mem_manager_type mem_manager_type;\\r\\n    typedef typename M1::layout_type      layout_type;\\r\\n    typedef typename M1::const_ret_type   const_ret_type;\\r\\n    \\r\\n    \\r\\n    cat_op( const M1& m1_, const M2& m2_) : m1(m1_), m2(m2_) \\r\\n    {\\r\\n        DLIB_ASSERT(axis == 0 ? m1_.nc() == m2_.nc() : m1_.nr() == m2_.nr(), \\"bad dims\\");\\r\\n    }\\r\\n    const_ret_type apply (long r, long c) const \\r\\n    {\\r\\n        if (axis == 0) \\/\\/either compiler will optimise this or branch prediction will\\r\\n        {\\r\\n            return r < m1.nr() ? m1(r,c) : m2(r-m1.nr(),c); \\/\\/i reckon branch prediction will optimise this, unless you\'re doing random access.\\r\\n        }\\r\\n        else\\r\\n        {\\r\\n            return c < m1.nc() ? m1(r,c) : m2(r,c-m1.nc()); \\/\\/i reckon branch prediction will optimise this, unless you\'re doing random access.\\r\\n        }\\r\\n    }\\r\\n    long nr () const { return axis == 0 ? m1.nr() + m2.nr() : m1.nr();}\\r\\n    long nc () const { return axis == 0 ? m1.nc() : m1.nc() + m2.nc(); }\\r\\n    template <typename U> bool aliases               ( const dlib::matrix_exp<U>& item) const { return m1.aliases(item) || m2.aliases(item); }\\r\\n    template <typename U> bool destructively_aliases ( const dlib::matrix_exp<U>& item) const { return m1.aliases(item) || m2.aliases(item); }\\r\\n}; \\r\\n\\r\\ntemplate <int axis, typename M1, typename M2>\\r\\nconst dlib::matrix_op<cat_op<axis,M1,M2> > cat (\\r\\n    const dlib::matrix_exp<M1>& m1,\\r\\n    const dlib::matrix_exp<M2>& m2\\r\\n)\\r\\n{\\r\\n    typedef cat_op<axis,M1,M2> op;\\r\\n    return dlib::matrix_op<op>(op(m1.ref(),m2.ref()));\\r\\n}\\r\\n\\r\\nvoid test_cat()\\r\\n{\\r\\n    dlib::matrix<int> a = dlib::ones_matrix<int>(3,2);\\r\\n    dlib::matrix<int> b = dlib::ones_matrix<int>(4,2) * 2;\\r\\n    dlib::matrix<int> c = cat<0>(a,b);\\r\\n    \\r\\n    cout << a << endl;\\r\\n    cout << b << endl;\\r\\n    cout << c << endl;\\r\\n}\\r\\n```","closed_at":"2021-01-02T16:37:26Z","comments":"4","comments_url":"https:\\/\\/api.github.com\\/repos\\/davisking\\/dlib\\/issues\\/2274\\/comments","created_at":"2021-01-02T15:53:46Z","events_url":"https:\\/\\/api.github.com\\/repos\\/davisking\\/dlib\\/issues\\/2274\\/events","html_url":"https:\\/\\/github.com\\/davisking\\/dlib\\/issues\\/2274","id":"777481219","labels":[],"labels_url":"https:\\/\\/api.github.com\\/repos\\/davisking\\/dlib\\/issues\\/2274\\/labels{\\/name}","locked":false,"node_id":"MDU6SXNzdWU3Nzc0ODEyMTk=","number":"2274","repository_url":"https:\\/\\/api.github.com\\/repos\\/davisking\\/dlib","state":"closed","title":"Feature proposation : dlib::cat for dlib::matrix objects","updated_at":"2021-01-02T16:37:2