{"actor":{"avatar_url":"https:\\/\\/avatars.githubusercontent.com\\/u\\/329682?","display_login":"sassman","gravatar_id":"","id":"329682","login":"sassman","url":"https:\\/\\/api.github.com\\/users\\/sassman"},"created_at":"2021-01-02T16:37:35Z","id":"14690747226","payload":{"action":"created","comment":{"author_association":"OWNER","body":"Thanks for the investigation and infos. \\r\\nI think at some point it would be worth to introduce a thread pool that is the size of the frame rate e.g. 10. With this thread pool we could achieve a stable capture rate since every frame within a second is captured by a thread of the pool. Of course this implies that one capture will never take longer than 1 second. So that in the best case only 1 - 3 threads will be busy at a time, and in the worst case 6-8 threads maybe remain busy.\\r\\n\\r\\nSo we got here 2 options.\\r\\n1. you could explore the capture via a thread pool to [be used in the loop](https:\\/\\/github.com\\/sassman\\/t-rec-rs\\/blob\\/main\\/src\\/main.rs#L182) \\r\\n2. you close up this PR by only adding a flag that translates to the 8fps that you have mentioned, and we are good.\\r\\n\\r\\nOf course option 1 would be more effort but might be an interesting challenge.\\r\\n\\r\\n(as part of #29 I\'m starting to use a thread pool for throttle like below via rayon):\\r\\n```rust\\r\\n    let pool = rayon::ThreadPoolBuilder::new()\\r\\n        .num_threads(10)\\r\\n        .build()\\r\\n        .unwrap();\\r\\n```","created_at":"2021-01-02T16:37:35Z","html_url":"https:\\/\\/github.com\\/sassman\\/t-rec-rs\\/pull\\/36#issuecomment-753495391","id":"753495391","issue_url":"https:\\/\\/api.github.com\\/repos\\/sassman\\/t-rec-rs\\/issues\\/36","node_id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDc1MzQ5NTM5MQ==","updated_at":"2021-01-02T16:37:35Z","url":"https:\\/\\/api.github.com\\/repos\\/sassman\\/t-rec-rs\\/issues\\/comments\\/753495391","user":{"avatar_url":"https:\\/\\/avatars2.githubusercontent.com\\/u\\/329682?v=4","events_url":"htt