{"actor":{"avatar_url":"https:\\/\\/avatars.githubusercontent.com\\/u\\/76782319?","display_login":"drsepanta","gravatar_id":"","id":"76782319","login":"drsepanta","url":"https:\\/\\/api.github.com\\/users\\/drsepanta"},"created_at":"2021-01-02T16:38:05Z","id":"14690749151","org":{"avatar_url":"https:\\/\\/avatars.githubusercontent.com\\/u\\/2131270?","gravatar_id":"","id":"2131270","login":"qbittorrent","url":"https:\\/\\/api.github.com\\/orgs\\/qbittorrent"},"payload":{"action":"created","comment":{"author_association":"NONE","body":">This also looks inconsistent to me. You wrap request\\/response data in JSON, thereby defining a new level of interaction between the client and the server, working on top of HTTP. Why do you still insist on using HTTP methods and status codes? Why not make this new protocol layer complete, and use HTTP only as a transport layer for it?\\r\\n\\r\\nThese are idustry standards. I understand if you don\'t see them necessary, maybe web socket is a better option for QBT, read below.\\r\\n\\r\\n>Please be more specific when expressing your ideas.\\r\\n\\r\\nOf course. I was suggesting to use a [web socket to transfer data](https:\\/\\/developer.mozilla.org\\/en-US\\/docs\\/Web\\/API\\/WebSockets_API\\/Writing_WebSocket_client_applications) instead of client pulling the resource every second or so.\\r\\n\\r\\n>Is this really required? Why would we want to open our resources to anyone\\/selected ones on the net? IMO, this is asking for trouble for our primitive web server...\\r\\n\\r\\nIt should be open so web app can be served on a different domain. There is no difference between the web-server you already have and a rest api supporting CORS. the only difference is how browser communicate with the server and giving access based on response headers. Server should send CORS [headers in response](https:\\/\\/developer.mozilla.org\\/en-US\\/docs\\/Web\\/HTTP\\/CORS#The_HTTP_response_headers) indicating who should have accees to the resource. Th