m\\/users\\/YannickJadoul"}},"issue":{"assignees":[],"author_association":"COLLABORATOR","body":"There are two changes in this PR:\\r\\n\\r\\nFirst, the setup.py code has been [modernized into setup.cfg](https:\\/\\/setuptools.readthedocs.io\\/en\\/latest\\/userguide\\/declarative_config.html), supported by setuptools since 2016. Note this _only_ affects the source; the wheel produced should be identical; neither setup.py nor setup.cfg even make it to the wheel. The declarative nature tends to be simpler and reduce errors, for example with reading files and decoding them.\\r\\n\\r\\nBy the way, you can remove the `setup.py` file entirely instead of leaving the simplest one (`from setuptools import setup; setup()`. I\'ve generally always left that file, but I\'ve been temped to remove it a few times recently because it makes people think it\'s okay to run `python setup.py <stuff>`, which it\'s not - that\'s an implementation detail. But [~~if I recall correctly~~](https:\\/\\/setuptools.readthedocs.io\\/en\\/latest\\/setuptools.html#setup-cfg-only-projects), you lose the ability to do `pip -e .`, since they haven\'t worked out a general form for that yet. So I left it here as usual for now.\\r\\n\\r\\nAlso by the way, the [PEP](https:\\/\\/www.python.org\\/dev\\/peps\\/pep-0631\\/) to allow this to be put into pyproject.toml was accepted provisionally, but hasn\'t been implemented yet in setuptools or other systems (and would require a really new setuptools for a little bit after it\'s in, of course).\\r\\n\\r\\nThe second change is replacing `-r requirements-dev.txt` with an extra, specifically `-e .[dev]`. This is nicer and more elegant than putting `-e .` inside a requirements file!\\r\\n\\r\\nI\'ve restructured the package finding around best practices - if we ever add a subpackage for example, it should get picked up now.\\r\\n\\r\\nTODO:\\r\\n\\r\\n- [x] Update bump version script\\r\\n- [x] Update .readthedocs.yml\\r\\n- [x] Update or add dev docs","closed_at"